digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56819-5
  rankdir="TB";
  "N1" [label="(1,0,0) Training and inference are two key processes in machine learning."];
  "N10" [label="(7,0,0) Each resistive state should not decay after the removal of the light pulse."];
  "N12" [label="(8,0,0) Floating-gate transistor (FGT) is an ideal device structure for NVOM."];
  "N13" [label="(9,0,0) Two-dimensional (2D) van der Waals (vdW) layered materials, with no surface dangling bonds, can reduce surface state generation, prevent carrier scattering, and enable fast device response, providing material base for FGTs."];
  "N16" [label="(11,0,0) In this case, the synaptic weights could not be modulated through light without gate voltage assistance."];
  "N17" [label="(12,0,0) The additional gate electrode poses challenges for large-scale integration and leads to increased electrical energy consumption and slow response speed."];
  "N20" [label="(14,0,0) To solve these challenges, we propose using 2D photoconductive material as photosensitive control gate, instead of the commonly used metal control gate, to construct FGT."];
  "N21" [label="(15,0,0) In this device, light plays a similar role to the gate voltage in traditional FGTs, and thus we call this device optical FGT (O-FGT)."];
  "N23" [label="(16,0,0) The 2D photoconductive material used in O-FGT should have low exciton binding energy (E_B) and long exciton lifetime (τ_0)."];
  "N24" [label="(17,0,0) The low E_B and long τ_0 allow the separation of excitons by the electric potential difference generated inside photosensitive control gate without the assistance of additional gate voltage."];
  "N27" [label="(19,0,0) Although 2D Pb-based and Bi-based halide perovskites have excellent photoconductivity, the large E_B and short τ_0 significantly impede effective exciton separation and transport."];
  "N29" [label="(20,0,0) We synthesize a dielectric 2D photoconductive material, (NH4)BiI3, which has high density of states (DOS), low E_B, and long τ_0 simultaneously."];
  "N31" [label="(21,0,0) The O-FGT constructed with (NH4)BiI3 exhibits adjustable synaptic weights using light without gate voltage assistance and achieves non-volatile storage, simultaneously meeting the demands of both training and inference processes."];
  "N4" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction-rule: If a hardware system needs to support both training (requiring dynamic weight adjustment) and inference (requiring non-volatile storage), then it must combine both capabilities in a unified architecture."];
  "N6" [label="(4,0,0) Utilizing optical signals for training and converting them into non-volatile electrical signals for inference combines the strengths of both."];
  "N7" [label="(5,0,0) This strategy leverages the wide bandwidth of optical signals and high-speed transmission capabilities, facilitating seamless integration with external circuits through electrical output signals."];
  "N8" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction-phenomenon: Optical signals provide wide bandwidth and high-speed transmission, while electrical signals enable non-volatile storage and circuit integration. Abduction-knowledge: If a system can leverage optical advantages for training and electrical advantages for inference, then it can achieve superior performance in both processes. Abduction-reasoning: Observing that optical-electrical hybrid approaches combine complementary strengths, and knowing that such combinations can optimize both training and inference, we hypothesize that optoelectronic systems are ideal for machine learning hardware."];
  "N9" [label="(6,0,0) Non-volatile optoelectronic memories (NVOM) are the hardware foundation for this strategy, expected to modulate a large number of non-volatile resistive states using ultra-short and ultra-dim light pulses."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes and evaluates an optical floating-gate transistor for neuromorphic computing by replacing the metal gate with a photosensitive control gate made from the dielectric 2D photoconductive material (NH4)BiI3, whose high density of states, low exciton binding energy, and long exciton lifetime enable synaptic weights to be written and erased by ultra-short, ultra-low-energy light pulses, stored non-volatilely across many resistive states, and used to combine optical training with electrical inference.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A neuromorphic-memory proposal is supported when material selection, device architecture, optical weight writing, non-volatile resistive-state storage, and system-level inference behavior are jointly aligned with optical training and electrical inference."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Because training and inference are both key machine-learning processes, a hardware architecture that can combine training-related dynamic weight adjustment with inference-related non-volatile storage is a plausible design goal for machine-learning hardware."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) The (NH4)BiI3-based O-FGT demonstrates optical synaptic-weight adjustment without gate-voltage assistance, non-volatile storage, ultra-low training energy consumption, up to 360 resistive states, high ANN task accuracy, and reduced array-level energy consumption, supporting its evaluation as hardware for optically trained and electrically inferred neuromorphic computing."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) For the proposed non-volatile optoelectronic-memory strategy, the device should modulate many non-volatile resistive states using ultra-short, ultra-dim light pulses and retain each resistive state after the light pulse is removed."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Two-dimensional van der Waals layered materials, which lack surface dangling bonds and can reduce surface-state generation and carrier scattering, provide a relevant material basis for fast-response floating-gate-transistor-based non-volatile optoelectronic memories."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Low exciton binding energy and long exciton lifetime are desired properties for the O-FGT photoconductive material because they allow exciton separation by the internal electric potential of the photosensitive control gate without additional gate voltage."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) The synthesized dielectric 2D photoconductive material (NH4)BiI3 has high density of states together with low exciton binding energy and long exciton lifetime, matching the stated low-E_B and long-τ_0 requirements for O-FGT photoconductive materials."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Conventional 2D-FGT optical modulation that still needs gate-voltage assistance creates a motivation to avoid additional gate electrodes, because such electrodes hinder large-scale integration and increase electrical energy consumption and response time."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Replacing the commonly used metal control gate with a 2D photoconductive photosensitive control gate yields the proposed optical floating-gate transistor, in which light plays a role analogous to gate voltage in traditional FGTs."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because existing 2D Pb-based and Bi-based halide perovskites have large exciton binding energy and short exciton lifetime that impede exciton separation and transport, the O-FGT design motivates photoconductive materials with low exciton binding energy and long exciton lifetime."];
  "N6" -> "N8" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "N7" -> "N8" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "N1" -> "R1" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "N4" -> "R1" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "N9" -> "R3" [label="induction-case"];
  "N10" -> "R3" [label="induction-common"];
